<h1>How to Improve your Local Vision Language Model</h1>

<p><i>TLDR: the best way to boost a VLM's accuracy is by using another VLM from a different model family to judge the original model's answers.</i></p>

<p class=note>What's the best way to boost the reliability of your vision language model? Average the outputs over multiple calls? use another VLM judge to verify the output of the original model? Give the model a zoom tool to better inspect the image? 
<br><br>
If you want to just have the best performance possible: no problem, just pay the $$ for a frontier model. Those costs can add up quickly though. On top of that, if you care about retaining your own data, then you don't want to let another company see your data. 
So that leaves us with open source, local models. In particular, I'm most interested in small, efficient models that can easily fit on a single consumer GPU (1B - 12B parameters)

<br><br>
<!-- fix link -->
Despite how frequently VLM judges are used in research and production, there is surprisingly little research studying how to make them as effective as possible. I am a fan of Lu et al's paper "When Does Verification Pay Off? A Closer Look at LLMs as Solution Verifiers"<a>https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02304</a> which studies the best ways to use LLM judges on text-only tasks.

I was curious about whether or not this finding would apply to VLM so <b>I spent over 300 GPU hours my local 3090's to generate these results</b>
</p>
<!-- note for later: migt need to rerun internvl zoom tool call? -->
<h2>Methodology</h2>


<p class=note>I tested <b>13 open source VLMs across 4 families</b>. <svg viewBox='0 0 48 48' class='lg' aria-label='Google'><path fill='#4285F4' d='M45.12 24.5c0-1.56-.14-3.06-.4-4.5H24v8.51h11.84c-.51 2.75-2.06 5.08-4.39 6.64v5.52h7.11c4.16-3.83 6.56-9.47 6.56-16.17z'/><path fill='#34A853' d='M24 46c5.94 0 10.92-1.97 14.56-5.33l-7.11-5.52c-1.97 1.32-4.49 2.1-7.45 2.1-5.73 0-10.58-3.87-12.31-9.07H4.34v5.7C7.96 41.07 15.4 46 24 46z'/><path fill='#FBBC05' d='M11.69 28.18C11.25 26.86 11 25.45 11 24s.25-2.86.69-4.18v-5.7H4.34C2.85 17.09 2 20.45 2 24s.85 6.91 2.34 9.88l7.35-5.7z'/><path fill='#EA4335' d='M24 10.75c3.23 0 6.13 1.11 8.41 3.29l6.31-6.31C34.91 4.18 29.93 2 24 2C15.4 2 7.96 6.93 4.34 14.12l7.35 5.7c1.73-5.2 6.58-9.07 12.31-9.07z'/></svg> Gemma at E2B, E4B and 12B. <span class='lgbg lg-qwen' title='Qwen'></span> Qwen3-VL at 2B, 4B and 8B. <span class='lgbg lg-gv' title='InternVL (InternLM)'></span> InternVL3.5 at 1B, 2B, 4B, 8B and 14B. <span class='lgbg lg-llava' title='LLaVA'></span> LLaVA-1.5 at 7B and 13B. Each model was run locally through vLLM on a single RTX 3090.
    <!-- add links to both datasets -->
     <!-- https://huggingface.co/datasets/vikhyatk/CountBenchQA -->
<p class=note>I ran all evaluations on CharXiv, a benchmark where the model has to interpret scientific figures. It's a great benchmark to determine how well VLMs can handle visual reasoning tasks in with real world data. I also tested on CountBenchQA, a very simple dataset where the model has to count the number of objects in an image. This is a very simple benchmark that frontier models crush, but smaller VLMs can surprisingly struggle with. </p>

<!-- make these images side by side and make it so caption width is image width -->
<div style='text-align:center;margin:2rem 0'>
    <div>
        <img src='figures/charxiv_ex.jpg' style='max-width:520px'>
        <p class=note style='margin-bottom:3em'>example charxiv question: <i>"at epoch 60, which training method has a higher adversarial accuracy?"</i><br>Ground Truth: Acccelerated DAT</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <img src='figures/countbench_ex.jpg' style='max-width:520px'>
        <p class=note style='margin-bottom:3em'>Example CountBenchQA question: <i>"How many spoons are there in the image?"</i><br>Ground Truth: 6</p>
    </div>
</div>

<p class=note>For each question, a solver model reads the chart and the question and writes an answer to the benchmark's question. Then, a VLM judge then reads the same chart, the question and the solver's answer, and decides whether to accept or reject it. Every one of the 13 model plays both roles, so I get a full 13×13 grid of solver and verifier pairings.</p>

<!-- link to paper with Lu -->
<p class=note>Following Lu et al, I run the VLM judge and the generation model in a loop with the where if the judge rejects an answer as incorrect, the generation model generates a fresh response to the question to try gain (capped at 5 retries). I measure the VLM judge under three different settings:</p>
<ol class=note>
    <li><b>Self</b> - model judges its own answers.</li>
    <li><b>Cross</b> - judge from a different family.</li>
    <li><b>Intra</b> - judge from the same family at a different size.</li>
</ol>


<p class=note>As a comparison, I also try two other test-time compute methods:</p>
<ol class=note>
    <li><b>Majority vote</b> draws 5 independent samples and picks the most common answer.</li>
    <li><b>Zoom tool</b> gives the solver a tool to crop and magnify the image, and is run with different maximum allowed budgets of 2, 4 and 8 total crops.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Results</h2>
<h4><i>Baseline - Raw Accuracy</i></h4>
<p class=note>In general across model sizes, Gemma is the best with Qwen and InternVL closely behind. I included Llava as a sanity baseline and because it is one of my favorite VLMs. However, its accuracy is... not great. 
</p>

<h4><i>Majority Vote</i></h4>
<p class=note>
Taking the average answer over 5 runs increases accuracy slightly, but is not really worth it given that you have to run 5 independent runs every time you want answer.
</p>

<h4><i>Zoom Tool</i></h4>
<p class=note>
The <a href='https://openai.com/index/thinking-with-images/'>Thinking with Images</a> report from the o3 announcment where they showed how training a model to use a cropping tool to zoom in on parts of the image gives huge boosts in accuracy is one of my favorite technical reports of all time. This cropping ability is now trained by default into all the frontier models. However, there's surprisingly little information out there about how to run open source models with the same tool and how it affects accuracy. 

<!-- note to logan, need to find a good example trace to show off here. LLM: don't worry about this comment -->

I think the reason why the open source labs don't like to highlight this ability while the OpenAI, Anthropic, etc all love to show off their benchmarks numbers (*with tools) is that the small models aren't good at consistent tool use. I saw small <span style='color:#1a7f37'>+2%</span> gains with Qwen and <span style='color:#1a7f37'>+4%</span> gains with Gemma<a href='#zoom-fn'>*</a>. InternVL consisently messes up the zoom calls and repeatedly zooms into irrelevant parts of the image and confuses itself. Llava was not trained to have multi-turn conversations with multiple images, so I did not test the zoom tool calls with it.

</p>

<details class=card>
<summary style='cursor:pointer;font-weight:600'>click to see InternVL zoom tool failures</summary>
<p class=note>Real traces from the largest model, InternVL3.5-14B (c8). It won't wrap its zoom calls in the <code>&lt;tool_call&gt;</code> tags the harness expects, so the crop never fires and the raw JSON leaks into the final answer; when it does crop cleanly it over-anchors on the patch and loses the global picture.</p>
<div class=card style='background:#fff'>
<p class=note style='margin:0 0 .3rem'><b>id 608</b> — Q: "Compare the value increase between 0-20 and 20-40 in subplot (c). Which one is larger?" (gold <code>20-40</code>)</p>
<pre style='font-size:.75rem;white-space:pre-wrap;margin:0'>…the segment from 20-40 K appears steeper th…
{"name": "zoom", "arguments": {"box": [0.55, 0.4, 0.75, 0.6]}}   ← untagged, never parsed</pre>
<p class=note style='margin:.3rem 0 0'>scored answer = the raw JSON. ✗</p>
</div>
<div class=card style='background:#fff'>
<p class=note style='margin:0 0 .3rem'><b>id 787</b> — Q: "At which time (in days) do all price trajectories … first reach a price of approximately 0.3?" (gold <code>60</code>)</p>
<pre style='font-size:.75rem;white-space:pre-wrap;margin:0'>…Zooming in on the area where the price drops to around 0.3 might help…
{"name": "zoom", "arguments": {"box": [0.5, 0.4, 0.7, 0.5]}}   ← untagged, never parsed</pre>
<p class=note style='margin:.3rem 0 0'>scored answer = the raw JSON. ✗</p>
</div>
<div class=card style='background:#fff'>
<p class=note style='margin:0 0 .3rem'><b>id 82</b> — Q: "What is the closest epoch number where a peak that reaches higher than 150 Fid values is observed for the baseline method?" (gold <code>200</code>)</p>
<pre style='font-size:.75rem;white-space:pre-wrap;margin:0'>&lt;tool_call&gt; {"name": "zoom", "arguments": {"box": [0.05, 0.05, 0.3, 0.3]}} &lt;/tool_call&gt;
"…the initial peak is at the start, so the epoch number should be close to zero…" → \boxed{25}</pre>
<p class=note style='margin:.3rem 0 0'>clean crop, but it anchors on the top-left corner and misses the real peak at epoch 200. ✗</p>
</div>
</details>

<p class=note id=zoom-fn>* To keep it fair I gave every model the <a href='https://github.com/LoganBolton/vlm-verification/blob/main/prompts/agentic_vision_prompt.md'>same zoom-tool prompt</a>. Gemma struggled with that shared format, so for Gemma I switched to Google's <a href='https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/capabilities/function-calling'>recommended function-calling format</a>, which is what gives the +4% reported here.</p>

<h4><i>VLM Judge</i></h4>
<p class=note>
By far, the best increase to accuracy was by using a VLM judge in combination with the orginial solver model. I let the VLM judge determine if the solver's reponse was correct or incorrect. If the judge determined that the original model's answer was incorrect, it would restart a fresh instance of the solver model to try again. 

</p>

<div style='text-align:center;margin:1rem 0'><img src='figures/charxiv_gain_by_regime.png' style='max-width:480px'></div>

